- canonical Mission Contract, Completion Contract, Workpoint, Trajectory, and settlement storage;
- organization-wide scheduling and worker orchestration;
- deterministic authorization enforcement inside execution services;
- credential vault/broker internals;
- independent verification services;
- durable memory promotion and contradiction reducers;
- distributed locks, idempotency ledgers, and compensation executors;
- signed organizational identity and policy administration.

Cockpit must display, request, explain, approve, pause, revoke, and audit these capabilities through typed contracts. It must not simulate conformance by storing local UI state that looks canonical.

## E.3 Conformance reporting rule

A release may state conformance only by domain and implementation level, for example:

```text
Mission Experience: implemented
Mission Contract UI: adapter-ready, backend contract pending
Deterministic authority: partial; ScopeGuard live, financial grants pending
Action receipts: browser/test evidence handles live, generalized receipt pending
Independent verification: test-specific only
Settlement: not implemented
ABPS maturity: Level 1 with selected Level 2 controls
```

“ABPS compliant,” “verified execution,” or “Level 3” MUST NOT be used as an undifferentiated marketing claim until every required domain is proven with release evidence.

